<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    How the launcher lists and sizes the widget.

    The widget is 4 cells wide, always: the one layout needs that width for
    the Rahao line and the two buttons side by side. At 3 cells the button
    names are cut. So it resizes in height only.

    From Android 12 the launcher reads targetCellWidth and targetCellHeight,
    and puts the widget on 1 row. Hark, 7 November 2026: at 2 rows it was "too
    big", 285 dp high on a Moto g56 for about 145 dp of content. A stretch to
    2 rows or more shows everything again. WidgetForm says what each height
    shows. minResizeHeight lets the launcher go down to 1 row.

    Before Android 12 the launcher works cells out from minWidth and
    minHeight (70 dp a cell, less 30), so there the widget still goes on 2
    rows, as it always did.

    The picker shows previewLayout on Android 12 and newer, which is a still
    copy of the 1 row form. Older phones ignore that attribute and show
    previewImage, a picture drawn by test/screens/widget_preview_test.dart.

    updatePeriodMillis asks for a draw once an hour, which is the most often
    Android allows without a battery cost. The draw reads the store and the
    clock only, with no network. It is what moves the date at SGPC midnight if
    nothing else has by then. The background refresh does the fetching.
-->
<appwidget-provider xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:description="@string/widget_description"
    android:initialLayout="@layout/widget_medium"
    android:minWidth="250dp"
    android:minHeight="110dp"
    android:minResizeHeight="40dp"
    android:previewImage="@drawable/widget_preview"
    android:previewLayout="@layout/widget_preview"
    android:resizeMode="vertical"
    android:targetCellWidth="4"
    android:targetCellHeight="1"
    android:updatePeriodMillis="3600000"
    android:widgetCategory="home_screen" />
